imes money and precious shell; from the Chou until the Ch\'in Dynasty they used cowries in trade.” Marco Polo refers to them in this capacity in his description of the journey he took into the southwest of China on behalf of the Great Khan. The I-shu (□) says, “Yünnan people call the pei ‘hai p\'a,’ p\'a being the shell of the animal.” There are in the Museum at West China Union University at Ch\'engtu metal objects made to represent cowries and once used as money. Hai-p\'a (Cowrie Shell) Miao girls also wear ornaments of silver in the form of cowrie shells.\'  This is in need of further confirmation.'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2344, 'temperature': 0.2})
